shape theory.chemistry.bio.protein : theory.chemistry.bio.amino-acids, theory.chemistry.bonding, theory.fractal {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// Protein Structure as Fractal Coherence.
//
// A protein is a polypeptide: a chain of amino acids linked by
// peptide bonds. The protein's function is determined by its
// 3D shape, which is determined by its sequence. Sequence is
// character; fold is emergent structure.
//
// Four levels of structure (fractal coherence, Theorem 7.1):
//
// Primary: the amino acid sequence. Character.
//   Determined by DNA -> mRNA -> ribosome translation.
//   A linear chain of 50-2000+ amino acids.
//
// Secondary: local folding patterns. Emergent from backbone
//   hydrogen bonding.
//   Alpha helix: backbone H-bonds between residue i and i+4.
//     3.6 residues per turn. Right-handed (physical chirality).
//     Stabilized by the regularity of backbone geometry.
//   Beta sheet: backbone H-bonds between extended strands.
//     Parallel or antiparallel. Pleated geometry.
//   Turns and loops: connect helices and sheets. Variable.
//   These patterns emerge because they satisfy Law 1: backbone
//   hydrogen bonds close. The alpha helix and beta sheet are the
//   only regular arrangements where every backbone NH and C=O
//   forms a hydrogen bond.
//
// Tertiary: overall 3D shape. Emergent from side chain interactions.
//   Hydrophobic core: nonpolar side chains pack together to
//     exclude water. This is the primary folding drive.
//   Salt bridges: opposite charges attract (Lys/Arg with Asp/Glu).
//   Disulfide bonds: Cys-Cys covalent cross-links.
//   Hydrogen bonds: between polar side chains.
//   The fold minimizes free energy = maximizes coherence.
//
// Quaternary: multi-subunit assembly. Emergent composition.
//   Multiple polypeptide chains in contact. Each subunit is a
//   shape; the assembly is an emergent shape with properties
//   none of the subunits have alone.
//   Hemoglobin: 4 subunits, cooperative oxygen binding.
//   This IS emergent composition (Theorem 6.2).
//
// Protein folding is the molecular version of coherence resolution:
// the chain explores configuration space and settles into the
// most coherent structure. Misfolding is incoherence: the protein
// fails to satisfy its internal reference closure (Law 1) and
// either refolds or is destroyed.
//
// Derives from: theory.chemistry.bio.amino-acids,
//               theory.chemistry.bonding,
//               theory.fractal (coherence at every layer)
  """
}
